// @emit-error field access but no struct type declared
// XFAIL, REJECTS TOO MUCH. A NUMERIC literal-union array is fine as a `const`, and refused in a
// function SIGNATURE. The string-literal-union spelling of the same program compiles and runs.
//
//   type K = 1.5 | 2.5
//   const xs: K[] = [1.5]          // fine — `xs.length`, `xs[0]`, all of it
//   function mk(): K[] { … }       // emitProgram: field access but no struct type declared
//   function n(xs: K[]) { … }      // same
//   function first(xs: K[]): K { } // emitProgram: index access but array type not collected
//
// THE CONST HALF ONLY STARTED WORKING RECENTLY, which is what makes the asymmetry worth pinning
// rather than shrugging at. `collectA`'s `f64`/`i64` rungs took `nodeTyArrayElemRepName(i)`,
// which answers `""` for a `TyArray` whose element is a `TyUnion` of numeric `TyLit`s —
// `tyIsLitUnion` demands `litKind == "str"`, so the numeric ones match nothing. The node fell
// through to `checkArrName` and was rejected. Those rungs now take the NAME as a second opinion
// (`arrays/elem-rep-name-silent-on-covered-nodes.vl`), which fixed every DECLARATION position.
//
// Function signatures are collected by a different route, and it was never fixed — so this is
// the same root cause surfacing at the one position the second opinion does not reach. Fixing it
// means teaching the signature path the numeric-litunion element rep, not adding another name
// fallback.
//
// The STRING control below is the whole argument that this is a completeness gap and not a
// deliberate decline: `type S = "a" | "b"` in the identical signature compiles, runs, and prints.
// Two literal-union element kinds, one of them arbitrarily unsupported at one position.
//
// ── RE-MEASURED 2026-08-23. THE PRESCRIPTION ABOVE IS WRONG, AND SO IS THE CONTROL. ──
//
// "Fixing it means teaching the signature path the numeric-litunion element rep" assumes such
// a rep exists and only one route misses it. It does not exist. `tyIsLitUnion` requires
// `litKind == "str"` — that is not an oversight at one predicate, it is the DEFINITION the
// whole rep layer works from, and it is what makes a string litunion `K[]` an i32-atom array
// (`nodeTyArrayElemRepName` returns "i32" through exactly that test). A numeric litunion has
// no atom rep to route to, so the signature path has nothing to be taught. This is
// **ROADMAP A16** — "the enum representation (i32 tag for a closed literal union)" — whose
// two owner rulings (`open-rulings.md` §A16-tag-scheme, §A16-ref-i31) are still open.
//
// AND THE `const` CONTROL OVERSTATES ITSELF. "A NUMERIC literal-union array is fine as a
// `const` — `xs.length`, `xs[0]`, all of it". Measured, verbatim, on the current seed:
//
//   type K = 1.5 | 2.5
//   const xs: K[] = [1.5, 2.5]
//   print(xs.length)        // 2      — fine
//   const a = xs[0]         //        — fine, binds
//   print(xs[0])            // emitProgram: print of a literal-union atom whose type
//                           //   carries no member texts
//
// The string twin prints `a`. So the element read is ALREADY half-atom in the declaration
// position the control calls working: one consumer classifies the element as a literal-union
// atom while `tyIsLitUnion` says it is not one, and the two disagree in the emitter rather
// than at a boundary. That is the same missing rep, one position earlier — which means the
// asymmetry this file pins is not "two literal-union element kinds, one arbitrarily
// unsupported at one position" but "the numeric kind has no rep, and the positions differ
// only in how far they get before saying so".
//
// WHEN IT CLOSES: it closes with A16's numeric-literal-union representation, not before.
// Then drop the prefix, flip to `@run`, assert `1` from `mk().length` alongside the control's
// `1`, and add `print(xs[0])` — the line the control claimed already worked.
type K = 1.5 | 2.5

function mk(): K[] {
  return [1.5]
}

print(mk().length)
